* Tuesday, 2011-09-06 at 08:14 -0400 - Ian Johnson <blindbravado@gmail.com>:

> Are you sure (iii) is even what you wanted? {di} seems to have come in fr=
om
> nowhere, among other problems.

Yes, that was deliberate. If the "zo'e noi" phrase is to truly be
a constant, it must not mention any externally bound variables. I was
missing a {zo'u}, though.

> Anyway, I don't actually know, but I also don't actually see how (i) and
> (ii) are semantically different, because I don't know how multiple {zo'u}
> work semantically.

You're right, they probably are equivalent ('probably' because it
depends on the semantics of zo'e, which are murky). I should have given
a more complicated example. Make it:

{ro da zo'u da brode lo broda be da bei de be'o de}

The options are now:
(i) {ro da su'o de zo'u da brode zo'e noi ke'a broda da de ku'o de}
(ii) {ro da su'o di zo'u da brode zo'e noi su'o de zo'u ke'a broda da de
    ku'o di}
(iii) {ro da su'o daxivo zo'u da brode zo'e noi su'o de su'o di zo'u
    ke'a broda de di ku'o daxivo}.

(Again, giving variables different names where possible, for clarity,
and explicitly putting in the {ke'a}s this time.)

